THEOREM A (finite-state pi are impossible; covers families (i), (ii), (iii)-capped).
Let s >= 0, let sigma: X -> S be ANY map into a finite set with |S| = K, and let
h: S -> (0, oo) be any table.  Then pi(n) = n^{-s} h(sigma(n)) violates P pi <= pi at some
m <= 3^K - 1.  Explicitly, with n_0 = 2^K - 1 and n_j = T^j(n_0) = 3^j 2^{K-j} - 1 (0 <= j <= K),
some pair 0 <= j < k <= K has sigma(n_j) = sigma(n_k), and the K rows m = n_{j+1}, ..., n_k with
multipliers y = 1 form an exact Farkas certificate.

Proof.  n_0, ..., n_{K-1} are odd (n_j = 3^j 2^{K-j} - 1 with K - j >= 1), and n_j >= 3 for
K >= 2 (n_0 = 2^K - 1 >= 3).  By (0.2) applied at each odd step,
    pi(n_0) < pi(n_1) < ... < pi(n_K).                                                    (A.1)
The K+1 values sigma(n_0), ..., sigma(n_K) lie in a set of size K, so sigma(n_j) = sigma(n_k)
for some j < k; write h* = h(sigma(n_j)) = h(sigma(n_k)) > 0.  Then (A.1) gives
    n_j^{-s} h*  <  n_k^{-s} h*,   i.e.   n_k^{s} < n_j^{s},
contradicting n_k > n_j and s >= 0 (for s = 0 it reads h* < h*).  QED.
Farkas form.  Row m = n_i (1 <= i <= K), unnormalized:
    (2 n_i)^{-s} h(sigma(2 n_i)) + n_{i-1}^{-s} h(sigma(n_{i-1})) - n_i^{-s} h(sigma(n_i)) <= 0
(the odd predecessor of n_i is n_{i-1}; note n_i = 2 mod 3 automatically since n_{i-1} is odd and
T n_{i-1} = n_i).  Summing rows i = j+1..k with y_i = 1 telescopes the middle terms:
    sum_{i=j+1}^{k} (2 n_i)^{-s} h(sigma(2 n_i))  +  (n_j^{-s} - n_k^{-s}) h*  <=  0,
every coefficient on the left is > 0 (n_j < n_k, s >= 0 gives n_j^{-s} - n_k^{-s} >= 0, and for
s = 0 the (2n_i)^{-s} = 1 terms carry the strictness), so the combination has all components
>= 0 and at least one > 0: a Gordan certificate for "no h > 0".  All rows have m <= n_K = 3^K - 1.

Corollary A1 (family (i), sharp single row).  sigma(n) = n mod M.  Take n = 2M - 1 (odd),
m = T n = 3M - 1 = 2 mod 3, (2m-1)/3 = 2M-1 = -1 = m mod M.  Row m = 3M - 1:
    (6M-2)^{-s} h(M-2)  +  (2M-1)^{-s} h(M-1)  -  (3M-1)^{-s} h(M-1)  <=  0.
Since (2M-1)^{-s} >= (3M-1)^{-s} (s >= 0) and (6M-2)^{-s} h(M-2) > 0, the single row is a
certificate: N_fail(M, s) <= 3M - 1 for every M >= 1, s >= 0.  Verified exactly for every M in the
sweep and every s in {0, 3/4, 1, 3/2, 2, 3} (field "analytic_single_row" in certificates/pi_farkas_modM_s*.json).

Corollary A2 (family (ii)).  sigma(n) = (parity_word(n, L), n mod 3^b).  Take
n = 3^b 2^{L+1} - 1 (odd, word 1^{L+1}: n = -1 mod 2^{L+1}), m = T n = 3^{b+1} 2^L - 1.
Then parity_word(m, L) = 1^L = parity_word(n, L), and m = n = -1 mod 3^b, so sigma(m) = sigma(n),
n < m: the single row m = 3^{b+1} 2^L - 1 is a certificate.  N_fail(L, b, s) <= 3^{b+1} 2^L - 1.

Corollary A3 (family (iii), capped valuations).  sigma(n) = (min(v2(n+1), K1), min(v2(3n+1), K2)).
Take n = 2^{K1+2} - 1, m = T n = 3 2^{K1+1} - 1: v2(n+1) = K1+2, v2(m+1) = K1+1, both capped to K1;
v2(3n+1) = v2(3 2^{K1+2} - 2) = 1 = v2(3m+1) = v2(9 2^{K1+1} - 2) (K1 >= 1).  Single row
m = 3 2^{K1+1} - 1.  (For the one-valuation automaton min(v2(n+1),K1) the row m = 3 2^{K1} - 1 already
works; with the second coordinate and K1 = 1 one needs the next shadow, since v2(3*5+1) = 4 != v2(3*3+1) = 1.)

