THEOREM B (family (iii), UNBOUNDED valuation weight; r64 s6 cascade made exact).
Let s >= 0 and pi(n) = n^{-s} g(v2(n+1)) with g: N -> (0, oo) ARBITRARY (not finite-state).
Then P pi <= pi fails.  Proof.  Fix L >= 2 with L odd or even (both work), n_0 = 2^L - 1, n_j as
above; v2(n_j + 1) = v2(3^j 2^{L-j}) = L - j for j < L, and v2(n_L + 1) = v2(3^L) = 0.
 (B.1) Chain (A.1):  (2^L-1)^{-s} g(L)  <  (3^L-1)^{-s} g(0),   i.e.  g(L) < ((2^L-1)/(3^L-1))^s g(0).
 (B.2) Row m = 2^L - 1 (its even predecessor N_L = 2^{L+1} - 2 is r64's cascade seed):
       pi(2^{L+1}-2) <= pi(2^L-1):  (2^{L+1}-2)^{-s} g(v2(2^{L+1}-1)) = 2^{-s} (2^L-1)^{-s} g(0)
       <= (2^L-1)^{-s} g(L),  i.e.  g(L) >= 2^{-s} g(0).
 (B.1)+(B.2):  2^{-s} < ((2^L-1)/(3^L-1))^s, i.e. (s > 0)  3^L - 1 < 2(2^L - 1) = 2^{L+1} - 2,
 false for L >= 2 (9-1 = 8 > 6).  For s = 0: g(L) < g(0) <= g(L).  Contradiction.  QED.
 Farkas form: rows n_1, ..., n_L (y = 1 each) plus row n_0 = 2^L - 1 (y = 1): the h(L)-coefficients
 cancel exactly (-n_0^{-s} + n_0^{-s}); the h(0)-coefficient is (2n_0)^{-s} + sum_{i>=1}(2n_i)^{-s} - n_L^{-s}
 which is > 0 as soon as (2 n_0)^{-s} > n_L^{-s} - (2 n_L)^{-s}, true for L = 2 (6^{-s} > (1-2^{-s}) 8^{-s});
 the intermediate even predecessors 2 n_i have v2(2n_i + 1) = 0, so all their terms sit on g(0) too.
 So the explicit family pi = n^{-s} q^{v2(n+1)} dies at m = 2^L - 1 with L = the least L such that
 q^L < 2^{-s} (single row (B.2) suffices there since g(L) = q^L is explicit) -- this is exactly the
 r64 s6 statement "penalising the -1 shadow moves the obstruction upstream to N_m = 2^{m+1} - 2".
 The general form of Theorem B (rows (B.1)+(B.2)) needs no knowledge of g at all.

THEOREM B' (two-valuation automata, unbounded).  pi(n) = n^{-s} g(v2(n+1), v2(3n+1)), g arbitrary.
Along n_j: state (L-j, 1) for j < L and (0, 0) at n_L; the even predecessors 2 n_i (i >= 0) all have
state (0, 0) [v2(2n_i+1) = 0, v2(6 n_i + 1) = 0].  Rows n_0..n_L with y = 1 give
   sum_{i=0}^{L} (2 n_i)^{-s} g(0,0)  +  (n_0^{-s} - n_0^{-s}) g(L,1)  -  n_L^{-s} g(0,0)  <=  0
   plus positive terms; coefficient of g(0,0) is > 0 for L = 2 as in Theorem B.  QED (L = 2, rows 3, 5, 8).

