THEOREM C (what any admissible pi must do -- the obstruction in one line).
If P pi <= pi and pi > 0 then along every all-odd segment n, T n, ..., T^L n (n = -1 mod 2^L,
e.g. n = 2^L - 1 or n = M 2^L - 1) pi is STRICTLY increasing:  pi(T^j n) < pi(T^{j+1} n), while
T^{j+1} n / T^j n -> 3/2.  Hence  pi(n) n^{s}  cannot be a function of any finite-state summary
sigma(n) (Theorem A), cannot be a function of v2(n+1) alone or of (v2(n+1), v2(3n+1)) (Theorems B, B'),
and more generally  g(n) := pi(n) n^{s} must satisfy  g(3^L - 1) > ((3^L-1)/(2^L-1))^{s} g(2^L - 1)
for every L, i.e. g grows at least like n^{s} along the -1 shadow: g is NOT bounded on the shadow
family for s > 0.  Since pi in l^1 needs pi -> 0, g must simultaneously be o(n^{s}) on average
and >> n^{s} along shadows.  This is C1/C5 in exact form.  The finite-N sweep below measures how
soon a finite table dies (N_fail), which is a different, sharper quantity than the analytic bound
3M - 1: the LP finds combination certificates at N_fail ~ M (s >= 3/4) and ~ M/2 (s = 0).

Status: Theorems A, B, B', C are complete (no missing lemma).  The hand bounds N_fail <= 3M-1
(A1), <= 3^{b+1} 2^L - 1 (A2), <= 3 2^{K1+1} - 1 (A3), <= 8 (B, B') are all exact.  What is NOT
proved by hand is the measured law N_fail(M, s) ~ c(s) M for the minimal N; see the sweep.

