THEOREM D (rigorous lower bound for the measured N_fail of family (i)).
For every M >= 2 and every s >= 0:  N_fail(M, s) >= floor(M/2) + 1.
Proof.  Let N <= floor(M/2).  Every number k occurring in a row m in [3, N] satisfies k <= 2N <= M,
and k = M occurs only as k = 2m with m = M/2 (residue 0, used by no other k in [3, M-1]).  Hence
n -> n mod M is injective on the set of occurring numbers, and the finite system on [3, N] is a
subsystem of the unrestricted system {pi(2m) + [m=2(3)] pi((2m-1)/3) <= pi(m)}, m in [3,N], on the
integers <= 2N.  The table h(n) := n^{s} 3^{-tau(n)} (tau = hitting time of {1,2}, finite for every
n <= 2N <= M -- a FINITE check, not C13) satisfies every row: the two predecessors of m each carry
3^{-tau(m)-1}, and 2 * 3^{-tau(m)-1} < 3^{-tau(m)}.  So the LP is feasible on [3, floor(M/2)].  QED.
Measured (sweep): N_fail(M, 0) = M/2 + 2 for M = 64, 96, 128, 144, 256, 864, 1296, 1728, 2048
(the first wrap edge 2m = M + 4 -> residue 4 at m = M/2 + 2, closed by the T-orbit of M/2 + 2 when
that orbit stays below M/2 + 2), and M/2 + O(1)..O(30) otherwise; N_fail(M, 3/4) ~ 0.9 M,
N_fail(M, s) ~ (1.0 .. 1.1) M for s in {1, 3/2, 2, 3}, nondecreasing in s (table in logs/frontB2-summary.log).
Sharp statement left open: prove N_fail(M, s) <= (1 + o(1)) M for s >= 1 (the analytic bound is 3M - 1).

